  user_input {
    user_response: "# STARFALL \342\200\224 Squadrons-Inspired Virtual Joystick Mouse Flight Control\n\n## Objective\n\nRedesign STARFALL\'s mouse flight control using a Virtual Joystick model inspired by the mouse control philosophy of STAR WARS: Squadrons.\n\nThe goal is to achieve precise, predictable, smooth arcade space flight.\n\nThe existing flight controls feel inconsistent and the aiming boundary feels too large.\n\nDo not continue stacking arbitrary sensitivity multipliers onto the existing system.\n\nInspect the current implementation and build a coherent input mapping model.\n\nImportant: This is a reference to established virtual joystick control principles. Do not claim to reproduce EA\'s proprietary internal implementation or unpublished numerical values.\n\n## 1. Preserve Existing Pointer Lock\n\nThe physical mouse edge rollback bug has already been fixed.\n\nPreserve the successful implementation.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Keep Pointer Lock API.\n- Keep movementX and movementY relative input.\n- Never use cursor recentering.\n- Never depend on absolute cursor coordinates for steering.\n- Continue receiving input when the physical mouse reaches the edge of the display.\n- Process each input event exactly once.\n\nDo not rewrite the working Pointer Lock implementation unless an actual technical problem is demonstrated.\n\n## 2. Virtual Joystick Architecture\n\nTreat the mouse as a virtual joystick.\n\nThe physical mouse generates relative movement.\n\nMaintain a virtual joystick displacement vector:\n\n- X controls horizontal steering.\n- Y controls vertical steering.\n\nNormalize the displacement relative to the configured joystick radius.\n\nThe virtual joystick must have a bounded logical displacement even though the physical mouse can move indefinitely through Pointer Lock.\n\nThe joystick displacement determines the desired steering input.\n\nUse a smooth radial response curve.\n\nFor normalized displacement magnitude r:\n\n- Inside the deadzone: output zero or a small configurable response.\n- Inside the active region: output increases smoothly with displacement.\n- At or beyond the joystick radius: output reaches its configured maximum.\n- Beyond the radius: output remains at maximum until the player moves the mouse back inward.\n\nDo not continuously increase sensitivity beyond the joystick radius.\n\nThis is important: the player should maintain continuous turning when holding the mouse outward, without runaway sensitivity.\n\n## 3. Initial Aim Boundary\n\nUse these initial dimensions:\n\n- Width: 30% of viewport width.\n- Height: 35% of viewport height.\n- Shape: Rounded rectangle.\n- Position: Centered.\n\nThese are initial STARFALL calibration values, not claimed Squadrons defaults.\n\nThe boundary should be smaller than the previous 40% \303\227 45% configuration.\n\nMake the dimensions configurable.\n\nHandle viewport resizing correctly.\n\nUse a mathematically consistent rounded-rectangle boundary.\n\n## 4. Sensitivity and Response\n\nSeparate these parameters:\n\n- Joystick Radius\n- Deadzone\n- Precision Sensitivity\n- Maximum Steering Input\n- Response Curve\n- Yaw Rate\n- Pitch Rate\n- Angular Acceleration\n- Angular Damping\n\nUse a smooth response curve.\n\nAvoid sudden jumps near the deadzone or joystick boundary.\n\nDo not use an aggressive quadratic acceleration curve that makes small corrections difficult.\n\nStart with a conservative response curve and calibrate through actual gameplay.\n\nKeep yaw and pitch independently adjustable.\n\nDo not assume horizontal and vertical sensitivity must be identical.\n\n## 5. Flight Controller\n\nThe virtual joystick produces normalized steering targets.\n\nThe flight controller converts those targets into ship angular velocity.\n\nUse a controlled angular acceleration model:\n\n- The ship smoothly approaches the requested angular velocity.\n- Small mouse inputs produce small rotations.\n- Large mouse inputs produce stronger turning.\n- Holding the joystick at maximum displacement maintains continuous turning.\n- Releasing or returning the mouse toward center smoothly reduces turning.\n\nAvoid excessive inertia.\n\nAvoid excessive smoothing and input latency.\n\nUse delta time consistently.\n\nPreserve the original Phase 1 arcade flight characteristics wherever possible.\n\n## 6. Camera Integration\n\nKeep ship rotation and camera follow separate.\n\n- Flight controller owns ship yaw and pitch.\n- Camera controller follows the ship.\n- Camera smoothing must not interfere with mouse sensitivity.\n- Avoid camera lag that makes the ship feel disconnected from the controls.\n- Preserve the existing third-person chase camera presentation.\n\n## 7. Settings\n\nExpose practical controls in the existing Settings UI:\n\n- Mouse Sensitivity\n- Joystick Radius\n- Deadzone\n- Yaw Sensitivity\n- Pitch Sensitivity\n- Response Curve\n\nProvide a restore-defaults option.\n\nKeep all values centralized in one configuration system.\n\nDo not create an unnecessarily complicated settings interface.\n\n## 8. Actual Browser Testing\n\nTest the actual game in the browser.\n\nVerify:\n\n1. Small mouse movements produce precise steering.\n2. The center deadzone prevents unwanted drift.\n3. Moving farther from the center produces progressively stronger steering.\n4. Reaching the joystick boundary produces maximum configured steering.\n5. Holding the mouse outward continues turning the ship.\n6. Moving back toward the center reduces steering smoothly.\n7. The physical mouse can reach display edges without interrupting input.\n8. Pointer Lock remains functional.\n9. Flight remains responsive and predictable.\n10. Camera follow remains smooth.\n11. Different viewport sizes preserve the correct joystick geometry.\n12. Performance remains stable.\n\nCompare the resulting feel against the previous implementation.\n\nDo not claim success based on code inspection alone.\n\n## 9. Scope\n\n- Focus only on mouse input, virtual joystick mapping, flight response, and directly related settings.\n- Preserve the successful Pointer Lock fix.\n- Do not redesign combat, missions, bots, weapons, or unrelated UI.\n- Do not introduce unnecessary dependencies.\n- Do not claim to have copied proprietary Squadrons code or exact internal values.\n\n## 10. Final Report\n\nReport:\n\n1. Previous input architecture and its problems.\n2. New virtual joystick architecture.\n3. Final boundary dimensions.\n4. Deadzone and response curve.\n5. Final yaw and pitch settings.\n6. Changes to angular acceleration and damping.\n7. Actual browser testing results.\n8. Remaining issues.\n\nThe priority is precise mouse control, predictable turning, and a smooth arcade flight experience."
    items {
      text: "# STARFALL \342\200\224 Squadrons-Inspired Virtual Joystick Mouse Flight Control\n\n## Objective\n\nRedesign STARFALL\'s mouse flight control using a Virtual Joystick model inspired by the mouse control philosophy of STAR WARS: Squadrons.\n\nThe goal is to achieve precise, predictable, smooth arcade space flight.\n\nThe existing flight controls feel inconsistent and the aiming boundary feels too large.\n\nDo not continue stacking arbitrary sensitivity multipliers onto the existing system.\n\nInspect the current implementation and build a coherent input mapping model.\n\nImportant: This is a reference to established virtual joystick control principles. Do not claim to reproduce EA\'s proprietary internal implementation or unpublished numerical values.\n\n## 1. Preserve Existing Pointer Lock\n\nThe physical mouse edge rollback bug has already been fixed.\n\nPreserve the successful implementation.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Keep Pointer Lock API.\n- Keep movementX and movementY relative input.\n- Never use cursor recentering.\n- Never depend on absolute cursor coordinates for steering.\n- Continue receiving input when the physical mouse reaches the edge of the display.\n- Process each input event exactly once.\n\nDo not rewrite the working Pointer Lock implementation unless an actual technical problem is demonstrated.\n\n## 2. Virtual Joystick Architecture\n\nTreat the mouse as a virtual joystick.\n\nThe physical mouse generates relative movement.\n\nMaintain a virtual joystick displacement vector:\n\n- X controls horizontal steering.\n- Y controls vertical steering.\n\nNormalize the displacement relative to the configured joystick radius.\n\nThe virtual joystick must have a bounded logical displacement even though the physical mouse can move indefinitely through Pointer Lock.\n\nThe joystick displacement determines the desired steering input.\n\nUse a smooth radial response curve.\n\nFor normalized displacement magnitude r:\n\n- Inside the deadzone: output zero or a small configurable response.\n- Inside the active region: output increases smoothly with displacement.\n- At or beyond the joystick radius: output reaches its configured maximum.\n- Beyond the radius: output remains at maximum until the player moves the mouse back inward.\n\nDo not continuously increase sensitivity beyond the joystick radius.\n\nThis is important: the player should maintain continuous turning when holding the mouse outward, without runaway sensitivity.\n\n## 3. Initial Aim Boundary\n\nUse these initial dimensions:\n\n- Width: 30% of viewport width.\n- Height: 35% of viewport height.\n- Shape: Rounded rectangle.\n- Position: Centered.\n\nThese are initial STARFALL calibration values, not claimed Squadrons defaults.\n\nThe boundary should be smaller than the previous 40% \303\227 45% configuration.\n\nMake the dimensions configurable.\n\nHandle viewport resizing correctly.\n\nUse a mathematically consistent rounded-rectangle boundary.\n\n## 4. Sensitivity and Response\n\nSeparate these parameters:\n\n- Joystick Radius\n- Deadzone\n- Precision Sensitivity\n- Maximum Steering Input\n- Response Curve\n- Yaw Rate\n- Pitch Rate\n- Angular Acceleration\n- Angular Damping\n\nUse a smooth response curve.\n\nAvoid sudden jumps near the deadzone or joystick boundary.\n\nDo not use an aggressive quadratic acceleration curve that makes small corrections difficult.\n\nStart with a conservative response curve and calibrate through actual gameplay.\n\nKeep yaw and pitch independently adjustable.\n\nDo not assume horizontal and vertical sensitivity must be identical.\n\n## 5. Flight Controller\n\nThe virtual joystick produces normalized steering targets.\n\nThe flight controller converts those targets into ship angular velocity.\n\nUse a controlled angular acceleration model:\n\n- The ship smoothly approaches the requested angular velocity.\n- Small mouse inputs produce small rotations.\n- Large mouse inputs produce stronger turning.\n- Holding the joystick at maximum displacement maintains continuous turning.\n- Releasing or returning the mouse toward center smoothly reduces turning.\n\nAvoid excessive inertia.\n\nAvoid excessive smoothing and input latency.\n\nUse delta time consistently.\n\nPreserve the original Phase 1 arcade flight characteristics wherever possible.\n\n## 6. Camera Integration\n\nKeep ship rotation and camera follow separate.\n\n- Flight controller owns ship yaw and pitch.\n- Camera controller follows the ship.\n- Camera smoothing must not interfere with mouse sensitivity.\n- Avoid camera lag that makes the ship feel disconnected from the controls.\n- Preserve the existing third-person chase camera presentation.\n\n## 7. Settings\n\nExpose practical controls in the existing Settings UI:\n\n- Mouse Sensitivity\n- Joystick Radius\n- Deadzone\n- Yaw Sensitivity\n- Pitch Sensitivity\n- Response Curve\n\nProvide a restore-defaults option.\n\nKeep all values centralized in one configuration system.\n\nDo not create an unnecessarily complicated settings interface.\n\n## 8. Actual Browser Testing\n\nTest the actual game in the browser.\n\nVerify:\n\n1. Small mouse movements produce precise steering.\n2. The center deadzone prevents unwanted drift.\n3. Moving farther from the center produces progressively stronger steering.\n4. Reaching the joystick boundary produces maximum configured steering.\n5. Holding the mouse outward continues turning the ship.\n6. Moving back toward the center reduces steering smoothly.\n7. The physical mouse can reach display edges without interrupting input.\n8. Pointer Lock remains functional.\n9. Flight remains responsive and predictable.\n10. Camera follow remains smooth.\n11. Different viewport sizes preserve the correct joystick geometry.\n12. Performance remains stable.\n\nCompare the resulting feel against the previous implementation.\n\nDo not claim success based on code inspection alone.\n\n## 9. Scope\n\n- Focus only on mouse input, virtual joystick mapping, flight response, and directly related settings.\n- Preserve the successful Pointer Lock fix.\n- Do not redesign combat, missions, bots, weapons, or unrelated UI.\n- Do not introduce unnecessary dependencies.\n- Do not claim to have copied proprietary Squadrons code or exact internal values.\n\n## 10. Final Report\n\nReport:\n\n1. Previous input architecture and its problems.\n2. New virtual joystick architecture.\n3. Final boundary dimensions.\n4. Deadzone and response curve.\n5. Final yaw and pitch settings.\n6. Changes to angular acceleration and damping.\n7. Actual browser testing results.\n8. Remaining issues.\n\nThe priority is precise mouse control, predictable turning, and a smooth arcade flight experience."
    }
  }
}
