  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Interactions**\n\nI\'m seeing a clean interface between `CombatManager` and `FlightController`. I noticed it calls `flight.getAimTargetPoint()` and `flight.getAimDirectionWorld()`. It avoids direct manipulation of `virtualAimX` or `STEERING_CONFIG`, which simplifies interactions. Now, the next step is a deep, methodical engineering.\n\n\n**Diagnosing & Designing**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in the problems of the previous input architecture. It\'s a stacked mess of unbounded accumulations and sensitivity discontinuities in `steering.ts`, `AimBoundary.ts`, and `FlightController.ts`. The sensitivity shifts are awkward: precision (0.5), a sharp \"dip\" (0.3), and then a ramp up to 1.8. It\'s time to devise an elegant, Squadrons-inspired solution. I\'m focusing on simplicity, responsiveness, and a more intuitive feel.\n\n\n**Refining the Sensitivity**\n\nI\'ve been wrestling with a particularly nasty sensitivity dip that made outward movement feel sluggish at the boundary before surging. The `virtualAimX` growth also created an invisible excess state, making control feel inconsistent. I\'m focusing on bounding the turn rate and ensuring it\'s cleanly capped at the boundary, without arbitrary multipliers. This includes fixing `steerX` which was unbounded, creating another degree of uncontrolled turning past the boundary.\n\n\n**Addressing Deadzone Design**\n\nI\'ve discovered a critical issue with the current deadzone implementation: it\'s axis-aligned, resulting in a square, not radial, deadzone. This causes unexpected behaviors. The aim boundary is also far too large, and the horizontal and vertical sensitivities are linked, hindering calibration. Finally, the flight controller\'s angular control lacks proper separation of acceleration and damping. I am working on a solution now.\n\n\n**Designing Virtual Joystick**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a complete redesign for the virtual joystick, starting with `AimBoundary.ts` and `steering.ts`. The goal is a mathematically clean, predictable system. The first step involves verifying the integrity of the `InputManager.ts` by checking the Pointer Lock API and handling relative mouse movements correctly. I will be preserving the `InputManager.ts` code, as it meets all existing requirements.\n\n\n**Defining Parameters**\n\nI\'m now carefully reviewing Section 2 and 3 requirements. I\'ve defined the initial aim boundary dimensions as 30% width and 35% height, using a rounded rectangle shape with a corner radius ratio of 0.30. It\'s centered within the viewport. Now, I am grappling with how the *Joystick Radius* should relate to the *30% x 35% Rounded-Rectangle Aim Boundary* I\'ve just defined.\n\n\n**Clarifying Relationships**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the interplay between the settings. I\'m making `joystickRadius` a unified normalized scale factor, defaulting to 1.0, with a range, such as 0.60 to 1.60. I also need to ensure that the `aimBoundaryWidth` and `aimBoundaryHeight`, alongside the `joystickRadius`, work in tandem within `STEERING_CONFIG`. I\'m using `0.30` and `0.35` as the base fractions for the width and height, respectively, and a corner radius of `0.30`.\n\n\n**Calculating Mouse Sensitivity**\n\nI\'m now calculating how physical mouse movement translates to virtual joystick movement. I\'m focusing on the effect of display resolution. It appears a larger display needs greater mouse travel to achieve the same joystick displacement. The next step is a design decision about storing the virtual joystick position: normalized space or viewport-scaled pixel space?\n\n\n**Evaluating Options**\n\nI\'m now comparing two approaches. I\'ve calculated the rounded-rectangle boundary in CSS pixels and considered mouse movement. The issue is applying `dx * mouseSensitivity` in pixel space, such that a 192px movement on a 1280px screen reaches the right edge. Now, I\'m examining a normalized reference resolution or direct pixel space.\n\n\n**Implementing Scaling**\n\nI\'m now implementing the scaling, which centers around the \"Mouse Screen Motion Range\" concept. A smaller joystick radius will result in quicker, more responsive full deflections, while a larger radius allows for finer adjustments. I\'m focusing on preserving the normalized joystick state across viewport resizes, similar to the `FlightController`. I\'m also refining `mouseSensitivity` to ensure the virtual joystick responds correctly to physical mouse movements.\n\n\n**Defining Maximum Values**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a critical concept: bounded logical displacement, as it is key to the Squadrons-style joystick. The aim now is to ensure the virtual joystick has a hard maximum, even if the mouse continues to move, preventing runaway sensitivity. This is vital for maintaining continuous turning without unwanted, over-sensitive motion.\n\n\n**Confirming Bound Behavior**\n\nI\'m now confirming the bounded behavior. I\'ve designed the virtual joystick to clamp at the boundary, not accumulate excess. Continuous mouse movement beyond the boundary maintains maximum steering. The instant inward movement, even small, smoothly reduces steering without delay, thanks to the clamp. This is key to preventing runaway sensitivity, even when the mouse goes far beyond the screen edge.\n\n\n**Verifying Joystick Behavior**\n\nI\'ve been simulating diagonal mouse movements against the aim boundary. The joystick\'s behavior has been clarified: it smoothly slides along the edge, whether straight or rounded. Importantly, the projection onto the boundary is smooth and continuous, eliminating sensitivity inconsistencies. This resolves the previous system\'s lag and sensitivity spikes. Now, I\'m analyzing the effect on continuous turning.\n\n\n**Deriving Displacement**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in deriving the exact normalized radial displacement on a rounded rectangle. I\'ve defined the necessary variables: half-width, half-height, corner radius, and inner dimensions. I\'m focusing on the mathematical consistency of calculating the exact ray-to-boundary distance and the steering direction. I\'m also ensuring the virtual joystick\'s behavior is mathematically sound.\n\n\n**Calculating Intersection**\n\nI\'m now determining the precise intersection point. Specifically, I\'m working through the mathematics to find how the ray intersects the rounded rectangle, accounting for both straight edges and curved corners. I\'m focusing on the relationship between the unit vector (`ax`, `ay`), half-width, half-height, and corner radius. I\'ve broken down the calculation into multiple cases: box intersection, straight edge hits, and circular corner arc intersections.\n\n\n**Validating Formula**\n\nI\'m now verifying the edge cases of the rounded rectangle formula to ensure accuracy and predictability. I\'m confirming that the calculated magnitude, `r`, correctly approaches 1.0 along both the x and y axes, ensuring the virtual joystick\'s behavior aligns perfectly with expectations. All cases must hold up!\n\n\n**Validating Direction**\n\nI\'m now focusing on calculating the direction and displacement vector for a non-square rounded rectangle. I\'ve validated the intersection calculation, confirming the radius calculation yields a perfect 1.0 along the entire perimeter, which means C1-continuity. Now, the goal is to create a complete and mathematically clean implementation with the proper direction.\n\n\n**Calculating Direction**\n\nI\'m now determining the optimal method for deriving the direction vector. I\'m evaluating two approaches, screen-space angle vs. normalized boundary coordinates, to ensure proper behavior in a rounded rectangle. My main concern is consistency at the corner, and I will check to see if the player pushes straight toward a corner of the 30% \303\227 35% rounded rectangle.\n\n\n**Evaluating Direction**\n\nI\'m now determining the most intuitive approach for mapping mouse input to ship control, and screen-space angle is far more natural. A true physical 45\302\260 diagonal mouse movement gives symmetrical yaw/pitch input, which feels correct. This avoids distorting physical angles, leading to a much better player experience. Now I am using this information.\n\n\n**Refining Deadzone**\n\nI\'m now refining the deadzone implementation, focusing on its behavior within the rounded rectangle. My goal is a small, proportional deadzone relative to the boundary shape and the normalized radial magnitude. I\'ve confirmed that a deadzone of 6% of the joystick radius translates to a small, crisp deadzone. There is zero discontinuity. This allows for smooth control and a responsive feel.\n\n\n**Verifying Response**\n\nI\'m now verifying the response curve math based on the detailed requirements. The core focus is ensuring a smooth, predictable transition from the deadzone through the active region and up to the maximum steering input. I\'m prioritizing mathematical consistency and a responsive feel, as detailed in Section 2 and 4.\n\n\n**Designing Response Curve**\n\nI\'m now designing the radial response curve, aiming for a C1-smooth function f(r) with a deadzone. Focusing on a `p=1.35` power curve, I\'m checking the output at the deadzone edge and small corrections for a precise, controllable feel. I\'m prioritizing zero discontinuity at the deadzone edge for a smooth takeoff.\n\n\n**Refining Control Curve**\n\nI\'m now integrating precision sensitivity with the radial response curve for nuanced control. I\'m focusing on blending `precisionSensitivity` smoothly near the center and ensuring it transitions toward 1.0 at the boundary. I\'m using a cubic Hermite blend function to achieve C1-continuity. This gives a responsive feel and clean blending.\n\n\n**Verifying Monotonicity**\n\nI\'m verifying the monotonicity of the response curve, ensuring the output increases steadily with input. I\'ve tested the function `g(u) = u^p * lerp(precisionSensitivity, 1.0, smoothstep(u))`.  It appears strictly monotonic for `precisionSensitivity` within the range of `[0.4, 1.25]`, confirming consistent control.\n\n\n**Validating Monotonicity**\n\nI\'m now mathematically proving the strict monotonicity of the two-term curve. I\'ve analyzed the derivative for worst-case scenarios and confirmed that, even with the lowest precision sensitivity and a linear response, the output increases steadily with input. This analysis confirms clean and predictable control characteristics.\n\n\n**Tracing Signal Chain**\n\nI\'m now tracing the signal chain, starting with raw mouse input, to ensure no redundant multipliers or arbitrary stacks. I\'m reviewing how the system handles yaw and pitch sensitivity separately, verifying a complete, non-redundant pipeline. I\'m writing down the process, step by step, for a unified signal flow.\n\n\n**Verifying Geometry**\n\nI\'m now verifying the geometry computations for the aim boundary. Specifically, I\'m confirming correct calculation of `halfW`, `halfH`, and `r_c` to ensure the rounded rectangle\'s dimensions are accurate and properly centered. I\'m focusing on viewport dimensions, joystick radius, and configured fractions. The goal is consistent boundary behavior on resizes and different aspect ratios.\n\nI am confirming proper positioning of the joystick when the user inverts Y axis, and the correct clamping based on radius.\n\n\n**Refining Steering**\n\nI\'m now focusing on clamping the virtual joystick to the rounded rectangle, not accumulating excess. Continuous mouse movement beyond the boundary results in maintaining maximum steering. Inward movement immediately reduces displacement. The math ensures smooth, C1-smooth sliding along edges and corners. My main concern is that even an edge mouse slide yields proper virtual joystick behavior.\n\n\n**Finalizing Rate Calculations**\n\nI\'m now integrating yaw and pitch with the virtual joystick for the FlightController. The primary goal is precise, responsive ship control, so I\'m using the `steerX` and `steerY` values as target rates, scaled by individual ship profiles, and confirming they respond predictably. I\'m focusing on verifying yaw and pitch behavior.\n\n\n**Calculating Rate Factors**\n\nI\'m now calculating the ideal rate scales, taking into account `yawSensitivity`, `pitchSensitivity`, and ship profiles. I\'m targeting a rate of 2.1 rad/s for yaw, which is 120\302\260 per second, or 3.0 seconds for a full 360\302\260 rotation. The existing system had sluggishness with the old aim boundary, so this is key to responsive control. This must feel awesome in combat!\n\n\n**Optimizing Responsiveness**\n\nI\'m now integrating all settings and controls to achieve the 1.5-second turn rate. Specifically, I\'m confirming that the yaw sensitivity range is [0.4, 1.8], defaulting to 1.0, and pitch sensitivity is [0.4, 1.8], defaulting to 0.90 for balanced vertical travel. I\'ll test these defaults to ensure a consistent feel.\n\n\n**Designing Angular Acceleration**\n\nI\'m now designing the controlled angular acceleration model within `FlightController`, addressing the key requirements. The goal is to achieve smooth transitions to and from the target angular velocity without excessive inertia or latency, all while preserving the original Phase 1 flight characteristics where possible. The angular velocity `\317\211` must approach `\317\211_target` in a responsive way. Now, I\'m focusing on the delta-time usage to achieve this goal.\n\n\n**Refining Velocity Control**\n\nI\'m now implementing angular acceleration and damping, carefully designing how `angularAcceleration` and `angularDamping` influence the ship\'s turn rate. I\'m focusing on a critically-damped exponential approach for smooth, responsive control. The goal is to avoid unwanted oscillations, and I\'m scaling acceleration by the ship\'s maneuverability profile.\n\n\n**Implementing Angular Control**\n\nI\'m now integrating the angular velocity control, focusing on damping and acceleration within the `FlightController`. I\'m optimizing for a crisp feel with no sluggishness, using a damping rate of approximately 14-18/s. The deceleration is sharper to feel tight and without overshoot. I\'m focusing on controlled angular acceleration ceilings to prevent snap jerks. I\'m ready to integrate Interceptor values.\n\n\n**Analyzing Angular Damping**\n\nI\'m now analyzing specific angular damping values to ensure an immediately responsive and controlled feel. With an acceleration of 18.0 rad/s\302\262 and a damping of 14.0 s\342\201\273\302\271, small inputs have smooth responses, reaching 90% in 0.16s, which feels silky. Full deflections have snappy acceleration without jerks, reaching 90% in ~0.18s. Deceleration with a time constant 1/17.5 feels similarly crisp.\n\n\n**Evaluating Camera Integration**\n\nI\'m now investigating the camera integration, specifically lines 70-115 of `CameraController.ts`. I see `cameraSensitivity` scaling `yawVelocity` and `pitchVelocity`, alongside boost intensity, for smooth, responsive adjustments. Heavier ships have naturally lower angular acceleration. The goal is a unified system.\n\n\n**Refining Camera Control**\n\nI\'m now refining the camera control logic within `CameraController.ts` to improve positional and rotational tracking. I\'ve noted that increasing camera sensitivity, intended to improve tracking, actually introduces unintended lag. The goal is to provide more immediate follow by tightening positional and rotational tracking. I\'m focusing on ensuring responsiveness.\n\n\n**Refining Camera Tracking**\n\nI\'m now refining the camera tracking to eliminate any lag when turning at the aim boundary. I\'m focusing on key parameters: reducing `lateral/vertical lag offset` from `0.65/0.52` to `0.50/0.40`. Also increasing `cameraRotationScale` from 13.2 to 15.5. I will also increase `cameraPositionScale` from 9.2 to 10.5. These changes ensure the camera seamlessly follows the ship.\n\n**Verifying Visual HUD**\n\nI\'m now verifying the Flight HUD visualization. The system draws subtle rounded-corner brackets precisely matching the rounded rectangle\'s geometry via `computeRoundedRectBoundary()`. This cleanly resizes with the new 30% x 35% boundary scaled by `settings.joystickRadius`. I will also render a deadzone ring at the center and a radial vector.\n\n\n**Visualizing Joystick Feedback**\n\nI\'m now implementing visual feedback for the virtual joystick, focusing on clarity and subtle cues. The reticle, tether, and boundary frame are being refined to clearly indicate the joystick\'s state. I\'m leveraging distinct colors and opacities to visually differentiate the deadzone, active region, and boundary, and ensuring the UI dynamically updates with joystick position. I\'m also preparing the DOM for automated browser tests.\n\n\n**Simplifying Settings**\n\nI\'m now overhauling the settings UI for the virtual joystick, focusing on clarity. I\'m consolidating the previous eight steering sliders into a streamlined \"VIRTUAL JOYSTICK FLIGHT\" section with the six requested controls. I\'ve designed a clear, concise interface, including a reset defaults button, for improved usability. Mouse sensitivity is set, and I\'m ready to move forward.\n\n\n**Refining User Interface**\n\nI\'m now implementing the redesigned user interface for the virtual joystick settings. I\'ve streamlined the settings panel with the six specified controls: deadzone, joystick radius, yaw sensitivity, pitch sensitivity, response curve, and mouse sensitivity. I\'ve added a reset to defaults button and optimized for clarity and ease of use. I\'ve also verified the responsiveness to physical mouse movements.\n\n\n**Verifying Config Integration**\n\nI\'m now integrating the updated `STEERING_CONFIG` with all required parameters. I\'ve ensured a complete and clean separation of *Joystick Radius*, *Deadzone*, *Precision Sensitivity*, *Maximum Steering Input*, *Response Curve*, *Yaw Rate*, *Pitch Rate*, *Angular Acceleration*, and *Angular Damping*. This guarantees control over the core steering parameters. Now, I\'m checking the source code to ensure that the code compiles.\n\n\n**Defining Configuration**\n\nI\'m now integrating the finalized `STEERING_CONFIG` with all core parameters. This includes aim boundary fractions, corner radius, mouse and joystick scaling, deadzone, precision and maximum input, the response curve, yaw and pitch rates, and angular values. I\'m verifying the clean configuration and ready to finalize.\n\n\n**Validating Parameter Defaults**\n\nI\'m now sanity-checking all default parameters in `STEERING_CONFIG` to ensure the final values are correct and consistent. Specifically, I\'m checking the default values for `angularDamping`, `stoppingDampingBoost`, `maxSafeYawMultiplier` and `maxSafePitchMultiplier`. I\'m verifying the boundary dimensions on different resolutions. I\'ve noted a possible issue with `mouseSensitivity`, which may need to be screen resolution.\n\n\n**Refining Sensitivity Scale**\n\nI\'m now implementing the normalized mouse delta scaling using a reference resolution. I\'m normalizing mouse delta to a 720p base and clamping the resolution scale. This ensures consistent virtual joystick behavior across different display resolutions. Now, physical mouse movement produces a consistent joystick deflection regardless of the screen size.\n\n\n**Validating Final Configuration**\n\nI\'m now validating the final configuration and verifying all default parameters for seamless, predictable control. The 6% deadzone provides stable centering on the 720p base and various screen resolutions. This maintains ultra-precise control for aiming, up to 4\302\260/s on the Interceptor, and the response curve allows smooth and responsive turning, up to 120.3\302\260/s.\n\n\n**Finalizing Controls**\n\nI\'m now verifying the final configuration and flight controls, including yaw and pitch rates. I\'m confirming that full joystick deflection at the boundary yields consistent maximum rates. I\'m also ensuring all default parameters have been verified for smooth and predictable control, especially under various resolutions. I\'ve confirmed a 6% deadzone for stable centering, as well as ultra-precise control for aiming, up to 4\302\260/s on the Interceptor, and the response curve allows for responsive and smooth turning, up to 120.3\302\260/s.\n\n\n**Finalizing Parameters**\n\nI\'ve completed integrating the finalized parameters, focusing on verifying the clean separation and accurate function. I\'m confirming that the calculations provide precision controls for aiming, along with all angular rates. Now I\'m working on full deflection at the boundary to ensure the appropriate rates.\n\n\n**Integrating Settings**\n\nI\'m now integrating the joystick settings, focusing on saving and validating data from `starfall.settings.v6`. I\'m updating `DEFAULT_SETTINGS` to include the new virtual joystick configurations and ensuring the smooth migration of old settings, including graphic, audio, and camera parameters. The changes need to be tested.\n\n\n"
    message_id: "jSz_avfAFIfRjrEP28-1qA8"
    tool_calls {
      id: "call_5956037906700599369"
      name: "replace_file_content"
      arguments_json: "{\"AllowMultiple\":false, \"Description\":\"Update SteeringZone, GameSettings, and FlightTelemetry types for the Virtual Joystick model\", \"EndLine\":185, \"Instruction\":\"Replace legacy 3-zone steering fields in SteeringZone, GameSettings, and FlightTelemetry with clean Virtual Joystick parameters and telemetry fields\", \"ReplacementContent\":\"export type SteeringZone = \'deadzone\' | \'active\' | \'max\';\\n\\nexport interface GameSettings {\\n  /** Global physical mouse-to-virtual-joystick sensitivity multiplier (0.30 to 2.50) */\\n  mouseSensitivity: number;\\n  /** Virtual joystick radius scale relative to the 30% x 35% centered boundary (0.65 to 1.50) */\\n  joystickRadius: number;\\n  /** Normalized radial center deadzone fraction of joystick radius (0.00 to 0.20) */\\n  deadzone: number;\\n  /** Independent horizontal yaw steering sensitivity multiplier (0.40 to 1.80) */\\n  yawSensitivity: number;\\n  /** Independent vertical pitch steering sensitivity multiplier (0.40 to 1.80) */\\n  pitchSensitivity: number;\\n  /** Smooth radial response curve exponent (1.00 to 2.40) */\\n  responseCurve: number;\\n  invertY: boolean;\\n  cameraDistance: number; // 7.2 to 13.5\\n  cameraSensitivity: number; // 0.6 to 1.6\\n  masterVolume: number; // 0 to 1\\n  graphicsQuality: GraphicsQuality;\\n  resolutionScale: number; // 0.65 to 1.0\\n  shadowQuality: ShadowQuality;\\n  particleQuality: EffectQuality;\\n  environmentDetail: EffectQuality;\\n  drawDistance: number; // 2000 to 5200\\n  antialias: boolean;\\n  postGlowEnabled: boolean;\\n  /** Development-only performance monitor toggle (hidden in production builds) */\\n  showPerfOverlay: boolean;\\n}\\n\\n/**\\n * High-frequency mutable telemetry updated every simulation frame.\\n * Read directly via refs inside HUD animation loops to guarantee zero React re-renders per frame.\\n */\\nexport interface FlightTelemetry {\\n  speed: number;\\n  signedSpeed: number;\\n  maxSpeed: number;\\n  effectiveMaxSpeed: number;\\n  throttle: number; // -1 to 1\\n  boostEnergy: number; // 0 to 100\\n  maxBoostEnergy: number;\\n  isBoosting: boolean;\\n  boostDepleted: boolean;\\n  boostIntensity: number; // 0 to 1 smoothly damped\\n  hull: number;\\n  maxHull: number;\\n  yawRate: number; // normalized angular velocity relative to base yawSpeed\\n  pitchRate: number; // normalized angular velocity relative to base pitchSpeed\\n  rollRate: number; // normalized -1..1\\n  /** Normalized virtual joystick deflection (-1..1 inside rounded-rectangle boundary) */\\n  aimNormalizedX: number;\\n  aimNormalizedY: number;\\n  /** Screen-space [0..1] position of bounded virtual joystick reticle */\\n  aimScreenX: number;\\n  aimScreenY: number;\\n  /** Live rounded-rectangle boundary geometry in game viewport CSS pixels */\\n  aimBoundaryWidthPx: number;\\n  aimBoundaryHeightPx: number;\\n  aimBoundaryCornerRadiusPx: number;\\n  /** Normalized radial virtual joystick displacement r in [0..1] along its ray to the boundary */\\n  joystickRadialMagnitude: number;\\n  /** Shaped normalized steering target outputs after deadzone & response curve (-1..1) */\\n  steeringOutputX: number;\\n  steeringOutputY: number;\\n  /** Screen-space [0..1] position of ship boresight */\\n  boresightScreenX: number;\\n  boresightScreenY: number;\\n  /** Active virtual joystick region (\'deadzone\' | \'active\' | \'max\') */\\n  steeringZone: SteeringZone;\\n  headingDeg: number; // 0..359\\n  pitchDeg: number; // -90..90\\n  rollDeg: number; // -180..180\\n  altitude: number;\\n  status: FlightStatus;\\n  pointerLocked: boolean;\", \"StartLine\":97, \"TargetContent\":\"export type SteeringZone = \'precision\' | \'transition\' | \'acceleration\';\\n\\nexport interface GameSettings {\\n  /** Independent precision-zone sensitivity multiplier inside the rounded-rectangle boundary (0.15 to 1.50) */\\n  precisionSensitivity: number;\\n  /** Sensitivity multiplier immediately upon crossing the aiming boundary (0.10 to 1.00) */\\n  boundarySensitivity: number;\\n  /** Asymptotic maximum sensitivity multiplier in the acceleration zone (0.80 to 3.00) */\\n  maxSensitivity: number;\\n  /** Distance scale S (in virtual px) over which acceleration ramps outside the boundary (80 to 500) */\\n  accelerationDistance: number;\\n  /** Nonlinear response curve exponent p in f(e) = e^p / (1 + e^p) (1.0 to 3.5) */\\n  responseCurve: number;\\n  /** Aiming boundary full width as a fraction of game viewport width (default 0.40 = 40%) */\\n  aimBoundaryWidth: number;\\n  /** Aiming boundary full height as a fraction of game viewport height (default 0.45 = 45%) */\\n  aimBoundaryHeight: number;\\n  /** Rounded-rectangle corner radius as a fraction of min(halfW, halfH) (default 0.25) */\\n  aimBoundaryCornerRadius: number;\\n  /** Ship angular velocity smoothing time constant in seconds (default 0.08) */\\n  angularSmoothing: number;\\n  /** Smooth delta-time reticle & sensitivity recovery time constant in seconds (default 0.12) */\\n  recoverySmoothing: number;\\n  invertY: boolean;\\n  cameraDistance: number; // 7.2 to 13.5\\n  cameraSensitivity: number; // 0.6 to 1.6\\n  masterVolume: number; // 0 to 1\\n  graphicsQuality: GraphicsQuality;\\n  resolutionScale: number; // 0.65 to 1.0\\n  shadowQuality: ShadowQuality;\\n  particleQuality: EffectQuality;\\n  environmentDetail: EffectQuality;\\n  drawDistance: number; // 2000 to 5200\\n  antialias: boolean;\\n  postGlowEnabled: boolean;\\n  /** Development-only performance monitor toggle (hidden in production builds) */\\n  showPerfOverlay: boolean;\\n}\\n\\n/**\\n * High-frequency mutable telemetry updated every simulation frame.\\n * Read directly via refs inside HUD animation loops to guarantee zero React re-renders per frame.\\n */\\nexport interface FlightTelemetry {\\n  speed: number;\\n  signedSpeed: number;\\n  maxSpeed: number;\\n  effectiveMaxSpeed: number;\\n  throttle: number; // -1 to 1\\n  boostEnergy: number; // 0 to 100\\n  maxBoostEnergy: number;\\n  isBoosting: boolean;\\n  boostDepleted: boolean;\\n  boostIntensity: number; // 0 to 1 smoothly damped\\n  hull: number;\\n  maxHull: number;\\n  yawRate: number; // normalized angular velocity relative to base yawSpeed\\n  pitchRate: number; // normalized angular velocity relative to base pitchSpeed\\n  rollRate: number; // normalized -1..1\\n  /** Normalized virtual aim offset (-1..1 inside rounded-rectangle precision boundary) */\\n  aimNormalizedX: number;\\n  aimNormalizedY: number;\\n  /** Screen-space [0..1] position of clamped virtual mouse steering reticle (strictly inside boundary) */\\n  aimScreenX: number;\\n  aimScreenY: number;\\n  /** Live rounded-rectangle boundary geometry in game viewport CSS pixels */\\n  aimBoundaryWidthPx: number;\\n  aimBoundaryHeightPx: number;\\n  aimBoundaryCornerRadiusPx: number;\\n  /** Outward virtual displacement excess past the rounded-rectangle boundary (px) */\\n  aimExcessX: number;\\n  aimExcessY: number;\\n  /** Screen-space [0..1] position of ship boresight */\\n  boresightScreenX: number;\\n  boresightScreenY: number;\\n  /** Active progressive steering zone & smoothed per-axis sensitivity multipliers */\\n  steeringZone: SteeringZone;\\n  steeringSensitivityX: number;\\n  steeringSensitivityY: number;\\n  steeringAccelRatio: number; // 0..1 overdrive intensity beyond boundary\\n  headingDeg: number; // 0..359\\n  pitchDeg: number; // -90..90\\n  rollDeg: number; // -180..180\\n  altitude: number;\\n  status: FlightStatus;\\n  pointerLocked: boolean;\", \"TargetFile\":\"/src/types/game.ts\", \"toolAction\":\"Updating game type definitions\", \"toolSummary\":\"Update game types\"}"
      thinking_signature: "\022\237\342\003\n\233\342\003\001i\024}\023\\l\316\214\272\212\354\260\370\030\343\263s\373\006jA\222\0236\272\207%\225\356>#\352\335\\\250<\244v\346\341\021\257\272\303\227\216\2637\261\223\263\024X\270\322\225F\340\314(@\306\306[\332\304\317\020\220r-%\326#\201\200\320\262\004\356}_\337\336\210\357&\217\272[\344\213\226\327\n\376\372Vf?M\vJ\020\351d\257\211\034c\324\306|l\n\177\343\006*t%\235\035\271\223\021Z\336\345\325\345\232*\227c\312\3317\274\257\003g\033\205=\331\346\202{\214\026\275F\035\340c\f\240\262\231\361j-Kn\211t\270R`\266(\315=I\227\263\034\200\355\305N2\003iQ\321B\236\207\323\375&|\271\327S\362\002\211\345`\347\223\252\237_-\251\255\250pO<Z\326\272\332\250\264\242\263%6!\366*\246\220\210\0045\331\373 \223h\370\006\304\223a\243\223\220\326\343\237m\342\232\264\024\304\246\264\2267\301\031\315\311\320\355g\260\265\325@\336\325\367\n\n\214Q\006\315\327\217\236|\357/fy\370\262\255\366Z\326c\361\221\023\336\024(\355,\267\3132\223\f\\]\376\317\311\376\333:\265{\267\031\001,c\210\327@b!\245\302k\242\033\177\"\355\347%\f\333\315\232*\251\240\346*\315\224\225\024\332\303G\030.\001/\326)n\035U$bH\317\353\246N\240\251\352.\'$\366\262\304y\'\b\305$\265\177_\303\356\177C\331\204\253\342\003\322\306s\320B\016 Z\365y\245\b\034\021\037\025\214\246\0218\203OV\330\327Q:\312\337?\342\272\023\024\346\263j\230>|~\345\031\301\213\244\347\250\206\325\361\231\"\005\277\027\222\324\313\272\v\021\366\360\034C\224et\027\316\306\025k\315\372\017{~\336}h%\266\377g\004\002\365b\032\264\234\265u\246\336\252z/\'\036\375\247\246\177y\314\202\3573|b\017\031\361C\245\250\fk\222\362j\206\302~\315\340\027\334\032\345Up\b\352\222\222\203\204\206w}QP\201\374\341!-5\303$\232\357\037\307\004>5\347\312bO\302\332\312\034\371\316>\211n\b\241j\323o e\a\365n\\\343\\\021U\216\202\262\232\270\214\275\371\2208\264\001\a\204\246\374\"\267%\033\t1T\330\320\377\355\302\0271Z\24057\275\002z2\024\237\345\316f)s\001\3106]\v\367p\247I\271}!{\377\231]\226\375vh\2235\231\242l\30